\title{The Tree Position of a Semiprime Is Adically Sealed}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Every odd semiprime $N = pq$ with $1 < p < q$ determines a unique \emph{Fermat pair}
$(m,n) = ((q+p)/2,(q-p)/2)$ with $N = m^2 - n^2$, and this pair is a node of the Berggren
ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples. The node's address --- the unique word in the
three Berggren generators leading to it from the root $(2,1)$ --- encodes the factorisation of
$N$ exactly. We determine precisely how much of that address is visible from $N$.
The address decomposes into two layers. The \emph{$3$-adic skeleton} (which coordinate of the
node is divisible by $3$) is a deterministic function of $N \bmod 3$ and conversely determines
it; for the Fermat pair of $N = pq$ the flag $3 \mid n$ is literally the trace condition
$p \equiv q \pmod 3$. The \emph{metric layer} --- branch letters, depth, path composition ---
is by contrast sealed: for every modulus $M \ge 2$ a single residue class of $N$ contains nodes
with all three branch letters and with nontrivial factorisations, so no function of $N \bmod M$
computes the branch letter; the same holds for the depth, and positionwise, for every depth $t$,
even conditional on the entire shallower prefix of letters. Two structural facts explain the
seal: the first letter is exactly the \emph{balance band} of the factorisation ($A$ iff $q>3p$,
$B$ iff $2p<q\le 3p$, $C$ iff $q \le 2p$), and the depth obeys $2d+3 \le m+n \le N$ with equality
on the $C$-spine, so the address is of size linear in $N$ and hence exponential in the bit
length. Any factoring method routed through this geometry must therefore either smuggle in the
Fermat pair (circularity) or pay cost comparable to $N$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Integer factorisation resists direct attack, so much effort goes into oblique attacks: rather
than computing $p$ and $q$ from $N = pq$, one tries to compute some \emph{partial} invariant of
the factorisation, cheap to obtain and informative enough to reduce the search.

A recurring source of hope is that the factorisation is \emph{geometrically} encoded in a rigid
object: the tree of primitive Pythagorean triples. The encoding is exact and classical. If
$N = pq$ is odd with $p,q$ coprime and $1 < p < q$, then
\[
m = \frac{q+p}{2}, \qquad n = \frac{q-p}{2}
\]
are integers with $0 < n < m$, $\gcd(m,n)=1$ and $m+n$ odd, and $N = m^2 - n^2$. These are
exactly Euclid's conditions for $(m,n)$ to generate a primitive Pythagorean triple
$(m^2-n^2,\,2mn,\,m^2+n^2)$, so $N$ is the odd leg of a unique primitive triple and the
parametrising pair --- the \emph{Fermat pair} of $N$ --- is the factorisation in disguise:
$p = m-n$, $q = m+n$.

The set of such pairs carries a perfect ternary tree structure: root $(2,1)$ and three generators
\[
A:(m,n)\mapsto(2m-n,\,m),\qquad B:(m,n)\mapsto(2m+n,\,m),\qquad C:(m,n)\mapsto(m+2n,\,n),
\]
under which every primitive pair is reached from the root by a unique finite word. Each semiprime
therefore has a unique \emph{address}, and reading that address is equivalent to factoring.

The question of this paper: \textbf{how much of the address is a function of $N$?}

\subsection{The answer}

Exactly the part that restates $N \bmod 3$, and nothing else. We isolate the $3$-adic skeleton of
the node and show it is informationally identical to $N \bmod 3$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:skel}--\ref{thm:resdet})
and, in the semiprime case, literally the trace congruence $p \equiv q \pmod 3$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}). We then show that every other feature of the address is not a function
of $N$ modulo anything (Theorems~\ref{thm:noletter}, \ref{thm:nodepth}, \ref{thm:properseal},
\ref{thm:posseal}). The seal statements are exact and constructive: each is witnessed by an
explicit family of nodes lying in one residue class and carrying three distinct letters. This is
qualitatively stronger than a statistical null: not ``no signal detected'', but ``no signal
exists''.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Node value]
For a pair $(m,n)$ put $\Nof(m,n) := m^2 - n^2 = (m-n)(m+n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fermat pair]
A pair $(m,n)$ is a \emph{Fermat pair} if $0 < n < m$, $\gcd(m,n) = 1$ and $m+n$ is odd. These
are exactly the Euclid parameters of primitive Pythagorean triples, hence exactly the nodes of
the Berggren tree.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Generators, words, evaluation]
With $A,B,C$ as above and root $r = (2,1)$, a \emph{word} is a finite list
$w = g_1 g_2\cdots g_d$ of generators, with evaluation $E(\varepsilon) = r$ and
$E(g\,w) = g\cdot E(w)$, and \emph{depth} $|w| = d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Branch letter and parent]
\[
\ell(m,n) := \begin{cases} A, & m < 2n,\\ B, & 2n \le m < 3n,\\ C, & 3n \le m,\end{cases}
\qquad
\pi(m,n) := \begin{cases} (n,\,2n-m), & m<2n,\\ (n,\,m-2n), & 2n\le m<3n,\\ (m-2n,\,n), & 3n\le m.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Letter at depth $t$]
$\ell_t(p) := \ell(\pi^t(p))$, with $\ell_0 = \ell$: the letter of the $t$-th ancestor of $p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Residue channel]
A function $f$ on the integers \emph{factors through $N \bmod M$} if $M \mid a-b$ implies
$f(a) = f(b)$. Such an $f$ is exactly what an adversary with access only to $N \bmod M$ can
compute.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Balance band]
For $N = pq$ with $0 < p < q$, the \emph{balance band} is the trichotomy $q > 3p$ (unbalanced),
$2p < q \le 3p$ (intermediate), $q \le 2p$ (balanced).
\end{definition}

\section{The visible layer: the $3$-adic skeleton equals the trace}

\begin{theorem}[Skeleton]\label{thm:skel}
Let $(m,n)$ be coprime and $N = \Nof(m,n)$. Then
\[
N \equiv 1 \!\!\pmod 3 \iff 3 \mid n,\qquad
N \equiv 2 \!\!\pmod 3 \iff 3 \mid m,\qquad
N \equiv 0 \!\!\pmod 3 \iff 3 \nmid m \text{ and } 3 \nmid n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Work in $\mathbb{Z}/3$, where squares take only the values $0$ and $1$. Coprimality forbids
$3 \mid m$ and $3 \mid n$ simultaneously. The three remaining configurations give
$N \equiv 1-0 = 1$, $0-1 = 2$ and $1-1 = 0$ respectively; the verification is a finite case check
over $(\mathbb{Z}/3)^2$ minus the forbidden corner.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Skeleton determined by the residue]\label{thm:skeldet}
If $(m,n)$ and $(m',n')$ are coprime with $\Nof(m,n)\equiv \Nof(m',n') \pmod 3$, then
$3 \mid m \iff 3\mid m'$ and $3\mid n \iff 3\mid n'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Residue determined by the skeleton]\label{thm:resdet}
Conversely, if $3\mid m \iff 3\mid m'$ and $3\mid n \iff 3\mid n'$, then
$\Nof(m,n)\equiv\Nof(m',n') \pmod 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both follow because the three equivalences of Theorem~\ref{thm:skel} are exhaustive and mutually
exclusive: skeleton and residue are two labellings of the same three-block partition.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact channel content]
The skeleton channel carries exactly the information of the trace set $\{N \bmod 3\}$: no more,
no less.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Skeleton $=$ trace]\label{thm:trace}
Let $p,q$ be odd and coprime with $1 < p < q$, $N = pq$, and $(m,n) = ((q+p)/2,(q-p)/2)$. Then
$(m,n)$ is a Fermat pair, $\Nof(m,n) = N$, and
\[
3 \mid n \iff p \equiv q \!\!\pmod 3, \qquad N \equiv 1 \!\!\pmod 3 \iff 3 \mid n,
\]
and moreover $N \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ iff $p \equiv q \pmod 3$ and $3 \nmid p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Oddness makes $m,n$ integers; a B\'ezout identity for $(p,q)$ transports to one for $(m,n)$;
$m+n = q$ is odd. Since $2n = q-p$ and $3$ is odd, $3\mid n \iff 3 \mid q-p$. The second claim is
Theorem~\ref{thm:skel} applied to the Fermat pair; the third is a finite check in $\mathbb{Z}/3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The single readable feature of the tree position is readable because squares modulo $3$ take only
two values --- a statement about the quadratic character of $\mathbb{F}_3$, finite in content. It
restates $p\equiv q \pmod 3$ and adds nothing.
\end{remark}

\section{The parent-interval law and exactness of the address}

\begin{theorem}[Parent-interval law]\label{thm:parent}
For every pair $p$, $\ell(p)\cdot\pi(p) = p$: the ratio $m/n$ alone determines the ancestry step.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Three cases: $A\cdot(n,2n-m) = (m,n)$; $B\cdot(n,m-2n) = (m,n)$; $C\cdot(m-2n,n) = (m,n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[No boundary cases]
If $(m,n)$ is a Fermat pair other than the root then $m \ne 2n$ and $m \ne 3n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Either equality gives $n \mid m$, so coprimality forces $n=1$ and $(m,n) \in \{(2,1),(3,1)\}$;
the first is the root, the second violates opposite parity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The parent is a node]
If $p$ is a Fermat pair other than the root, $\pi(p)$ is a Fermat pair.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Ordering follows from the band inequalities and the previous lemma; coprimality is preserved
because each branch is an elementary transformation $a \mapsto a+kb$ up to swap and sign; opposite
parity is preserved because $m+n$ changes by an even amount.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of parent and letter]
Let $p$ be a Fermat pair other than the root. If $g\cdot q = p$ for a valid pair $q$ and a
generator $g$, then $g = \ell(p)$ and $q = \pi(p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:parent}, $g\cdot q = \ell(p)\cdot\pi(p)$. The generators are injective on
valid pairs and their images are separated by the bands ($A$ into $m<2n$, $B$ into $2n<m<3n$,
$C$ into $m>3n$), so $g = \ell(p)$ and then $q = \pi(p)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strict descent]
For a Fermat pair $p$, $\pi(p)_1 + \pi(p)_2 < p_1 + p_2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Unique address; the tree is exactly the Fermat pairs]\label{thm:bij}
For every Fermat pair $p$ there is a unique word $w$ with $E(w) = p$, and $E(w)$ is a Fermat pair
for every $w$. Hence $E:\{A,B,C\}^\ast \to \{\text{Fermat pairs}\}$ is a bijection.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Existence by strong induction on $p_1+p_2$ using the strict descent and the parent-interval law;
uniqueness by iterated application of the uniqueness of the parent (freeness of the action); the
converse by induction on $|w|$, each generator preserving all four defining conditions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The address is the factorisation]\label{thm:addrfact}
For any nonempty word $w$ with $E(w) = (m,n)$ one has $\Nof(m,n) = (m-n)(m+n)$ with $m-n > 0$ and
$m+n > 2$. Knowledge of the address yields a nontrivial factorisation of $N$; for the Fermat pair
of a semiprime the two factors are exactly $p$ and $q$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The seal, first form: no residue channel gives the letter}

\begin{theorem}[Three letters in one residue class]\label{thm:threeletters}
Let $M \ge 3$ be odd. The pairs $w_A = (M+1,M)$, $w_B = (3M-1,M)$, $w_C = (3M+1,M)$ are Fermat
pairs with
\[
\Nof(w_A) = 2M+1,\quad \Nof(w_B) = 8M^2-6M+1,\quad \Nof(w_C) = 8M^2+6M+1,
\]
all congruent to $1$ modulo $M$, and with letters $A$, $B$, $C$ respectively.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Ordering is clear for $M \ge 3$; coprimality is witnessed by $1\cdot(M+1)-1\cdot M = 1$,
$-1\cdot(3M-1)+3\cdot M = 1$, $1\cdot(3M+1)-3\cdot M = 1$; opposite parity holds since $M$ is odd.
The $\Nof$-values are direct expansions, each $1$ plus a multiple of $M$. The bands:
$M+1 < 2M$; $2M \le 3M-1 < 3M$; $3M \le 3M+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No letter function]\label{thm:noletter}
Let $M \ge 3$ be odd and let $f$ factor through $N \bmod M$. Then there is a Fermat pair $p$ with
$f(\Nof(p)) \ne \ell(p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Correctness on $w_A$ and $w_B$, whose $\Nof$-values are congruent mod $M$, would give $A = B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$3$-adic form]
For every $k \ge 1$ and every $f$ factoring through $N \bmod 3^k$ there is a Fermat pair on which
$f$ fails to compute the letter; equivalently, at every level $3^k$ there are three Fermat pairs
with pairwise congruent $N$-values and letters $A$, $B$, $C$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Depth blindness and the size of the channel}

\begin{lemma}[Spine evaluation]\label{lem:spine}
$E(C^d) = (2+2d,\,1)$, so $\Nof(E(C^d)) = (2+2d)^2 - 1 = (2d+1)(2d+3)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded depth in one residue class]
For every $M \ge 1$ and $j \ge 0$, $M \mid \Nof(E(C^{1+jM})) - \Nof(E(C^1))$, while the depths are
$1+jM$ and $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:spine}, $\Nof(E(C^{1+jM})) = (4+2jM)^2 - 1 = 15 + M(16j+4j^2M)$ and
$\Nof(E(C^1)) = 15$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No depth function]\label{thm:nodepth}
For every $M \ge 1$ and every $f$ factoring through $N \bmod M$ there is a word $w$ with
$f(\Nof(E(w))) \ne |w|$; in particular at every $3$-adic level.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Linear depth bound]\label{thm:depthbound}
For every word $w$ with $E(w) = (m,n)$ one has $2|w| + 3 \le m+n$, with equality exactly on the
$C$-spine.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: at the root $3 = 2+1$; and each generator increases $m+n$ by $2m-2n$, $2m$ or $2n$,
each at least $2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exponential in the bit length]\label{cor:expo}
For every nonempty word $w$, $2|w|+3 \le \Nof(E(w))$. The depth of the $N$-node can be
$\Theta(N)$, i.e.\ exponential in $\log N$, attained on the $C$-spine; writing the address down is
not a polynomial-size operation.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The tree position is adically sealed]
For every $k \ge 1$: (i) the skeleton flag $3 \mid n$ is a function of $N \bmod 3$; (ii) no
function factoring through $N \bmod 3^k$ computes the branch letter on all Fermat pairs; (iii) no
function factoring through $N \bmod 3^k$ computes the depth on all words.
\end{theorem}

\section{The strengthened seal}

\subsection{Nontrivial factorisations, all moduli}

The $A$-witness $(M+1,M)$ above has $m-n = 1$, encoding the trivial factorisation
$N = 1 \cdot N$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:threeletters} assumed $M$ odd. Both weaknesses are
removable at once.

\begin{theorem}[Proper witnesses]\label{thm:proper}
Let $M \ge 2$ be any integer. The pairs $W_A = (4M-1,2M)$, $W_B = (4M+1,2M)$, $W_C = (6M+1,2M)$
are Fermat pairs with
\[
\Nof(W_A) = (2M-1)(6M-1),\quad \Nof(W_B) = (2M+1)(6M+1),\quad \Nof(W_C) = (4M+1)(8M+1),
\]
all congruent to $1$ modulo $M$; each has $m-n \ge 2M-1 \ge 3$, so the exhibited factorisation is
nontrivial; and the letters are $A$, $B$, $C$ respectively.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Coprimality: $-1\cdot(4M-1)+2\cdot 2M = 1$, $1\cdot(4M+1)-2\cdot 2M = 1$,
$1\cdot(6M+1)-3\cdot 2M = 1$. Parity: $m$ odd, $n = 2M$ even. Expansions:
$12M^2-8M+1$, $12M^2+8M+1$, $32M^2+12M+1$, each $\equiv 1 \pmod M$. Bands: $4M-1 < 4M$;
$4M \le 4M+1 < 6M$ for $M \ge 1$; $6M \le 6M+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No letter function, proper form]\label{thm:properseal}
For every $M \ge 2$, odd or even, and every $f$ factoring through $N \bmod M$, there is a Fermat
pair $p$ with $m-n \ge 3$ and $f(\Nof(p)) \ne \ell(p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Balance band not computable]\label{thm:balanceseal}
For every $M \ge 2$ there are Fermat pairs $p_1,p_2$ with congruent $N$-values modulo $M$ such
that $p_1$ is unbalanced, $3(m_1-n_1) < m_1+n_1$, and $p_2$ is balanced,
$m_2+n_2 \le 2(m_2-n_2)$. No function of $N \bmod M$ separates them.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $p_1 = W_A$, $p_2 = W_C$: $6M-3 < 6M-1$ and $8M+1 \le 8M+2$, while
$\Nof(W_A)-\Nof(W_C) = -20M^2-20M$ is divisible by $M$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Positionwise: even the deep letters are sealed}

\begin{definition}
For $M \ge 2$, $k \in \{1,2,3\}$, $t \ge 0$ put $\nu(M,k,t) := (4tM + 2Mk + 1,\; 2M)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:nu}
$\nu(M,k,t)$ is a Fermat pair, $\Nof(\nu(M,k,t)) \equiv 1 \pmod M$, and for $M,k \ge 1$,
$\pi(\nu(M,k,t+1)) = \nu(M,k,t)$, hence $\pi^{r}(\nu(M,k,r+u)) = \nu(M,k,u)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Coprimality: $1\cdot m - (2t+k)\cdot n = 1$. Parity: $m$ odd, $n$ even. The residue claim is an
expansion. For the descent, $m = 4(t+1)M+2Mk+1 \ge 6M$ puts the node in the $C$-band, where $\pi$
subtracts $2n = 4M$ from $m$: exactly the step from $t+1$ to $t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[All letters at depth $t$ in one residue class]\label{thm:depthwise}
For every $M \ge 2$ and $t \ge 0$ the three nodes $\nu(M,1,t), \nu(M,2,t), \nu(M,3,t)$ all satisfy
$N \equiv 1 \pmod M$; all have $\ell_s = C$ for every $s < t$ (they agree in the entire shallower
prefix); and their letters at depth $t$ are $A$, $B$, $C$ respectively.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:nu}, $\pi^s(\nu(M,k,t)) = \nu(M,k,t-s)$, whose first coordinate is $\ge 6M$ when
$t-s \ge 1$, giving letter $C$. At $s = t$ the node is $(2Mk+1,2M)$, and $2M+1 < 4M$,
$4M \le 4M+1 < 6M$, $6M \le 6M+1$ give $A$, $B$, $C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positionwise seal]\label{thm:posseal}
Let $M \ge 2$ and $t \ge 0$, and let $f(N,u)$ be any function of $N \bmod M$ together with a
prefix $u$ of letters, in the sense that $f(a,u) = f(b,v)$ whenever $M \mid a-b$ and $u_s = v_s$
for all $s<t$. Then there is a Fermat pair $p$ with
$f(\Nof(p),(\ell_s(p))_s) \ne \ell_t(p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The nodes $\nu(M,1,t)$ and $\nu(M,2,t)$ have congruent $N$-values and identical prefixes, so $f$
returns the same value on both; their letters at depth $t$ are $A$ and $B$.
\end{proof}

\section{Why the seal matters: the letter is the balance band}

\begin{theorem}[Letter $=$ balance band]\label{thm:balance}
Let $p,q$ be odd with $0 < p < q$ and let $(m,n) = ((q+p)/2,(q-p)/2)$ be the Fermat pair of
$N = pq$. Then
\[
\ell(m,n) = A \iff q > 3p, \qquad \ell(m,n) = B \iff 2p < q \le 3p, \qquad \ell(m,n) = C \iff q \le 2p.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substituting, $m < 2n$ becomes $q+p < 2(q-p)$, i.e.\ $3p<q$; and $m<3n$ becomes $q+p<3(q-p)$,
i.e.\ $2p<q$. The trichotomy follows.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Determinism of the band-to-letter map]
Two nodes agreeing on the truth values of $m<2n$ and $m<3n$ have the same letter.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Reading the band is reading the factorisation]\label{thm:circ}
For a Fermat pair $(m,n)$, $\Nof(m,n) = (m-n)(m+n)$ with $m-n>0$ and $m+n>1$: possession of the
node hands over a nontrivial factorisation of $N$.
\end{theorem}

Theorems~\ref{thm:balance}--\ref{thm:circ} are the structural reason the seal must hold, or, read
from the other side, the reason that breaking it would be a factoring algorithm.

\section{Empirical corroboration}

A computational study preceded and corroborates the theorems. The sample was $40{,}000$
semiprimes $N = pq$ with $p,q$ independent uniform primes in $[2^{16},2^{24})$, primality by
Miller--Rabin with $12$ bases, fixed seeds, descents capped at $5000$ steps; twin-type nodes with
$n=1$ descend one unit at a time and exhaust the cap, and were censored and reported ($42$ of
$40402$ trials, $0.10\%$). Runtime $154$\,s.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \emph{Skeleton and trace.} Agreement $40000/40000$ on the three-way skeleton equivalence and
on $3\mid n \iff q \equiv p \pmod 3 \iff N \equiv 1 \pmod 3$.
\item \emph{Parent law and exactness.} All $40{,}000$ descents terminated exactly at the root
$(2,1)$; on $86{,}634$ spot-checked steps the band-decided parent and the child-map reconstruction
agreed exactly.
\item \emph{Blindness.} Mutual information $I(N \bmod 3^k; \ell_t)$ against a $300$-shuffle
permutation null for all $t \le 10$, $k \le 6$, and likewise for depth and letter composition:
across $\approx 150$ tests the worst deviation was $z = +2.51$, within noise before multiplicity
correction. Flagship cell: $I(N \bmod 3; \ell_0) = 0.00004$ bits versus null $0.00004$,
$z = +0.04$.
\item \emph{Positive controls.} $I(N \bmod 3;\, s \bmod 3) = 1.0000$ bits exactly for the factor
sum $s$; $I(\text{ratio band};\,\ell_0) = 1.4738$ bits $= H(\ell_0)$ exactly; and
$\mathrm{corr}(\log d_B, \log(q-p)) = -0.141$.
\end{itemize}

Every empirical finding is subsumed by a theorem above, and every theorem strengthens the
corresponding empirical claim from ``not detected at tested levels'' to ``does not exist at any
level''.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{The dichotomy}

Any proposed factoring method operating by reading the Berggren position of the $N$-node faces a
dichotomy. \emph{Circularity}: if it obtains the letters, the balance band, or the ratio $m/n$,
then by Theorem~\ref{thm:circ} it already has the Fermat pair, hence the factorisation --- and it
cannot obtain them from residues of $N$, by Theorems~\ref{thm:properseal} and \ref{thm:posseal},
at any modulus, any position, even given all shallower letters. \emph{Cost}: if instead it
traverses the tree, Corollary~\ref{cor:expo} makes the address of size $\Theta(N)$ in the worst
case, exponential in $\log N$, attained on the $C$-spine. There is no third option in this
geometry.

\subsection{What the seal does not say}

The seal concerns \emph{residue channels}: functions of $N \bmod M$. It does not rule out channels
of other kinds (lattice, analytic, quantum). It is a worst-case correctness statement: such a
function must be wrong somewhere; the witnesses are however dense families parametrised by $M$ and
$t$, and the empirical mutual information of $\approx 4\times 10^{-5}$ bits indicates no
exploitable average-case bias either. Finally, the skeleton \emph{is} a real channel --- merely a
redundant one. The correct summary is not that the tree says nothing, but that it says
$N \bmod 3$, twice.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
\item \emph{Other trees.} Barning's matrix tree, the Price tree and the Fibonacci-box tree give
alternative ternary structures on primitive triples. Do the analogous position channels seal in
the same way, and is there a uniform proof?
\item \emph{Beyond residue channels.} Formulate and settle the analogous seal for lattice-type
channels: is the tree position invisible to a short-vector oracle on a lattice built from $N$?
\item \emph{Quantitative average-case seal.} Bound the statistical distance between the joint law
of $(N \bmod M, \ell_t)$ and the product of its marginals for random semiprimes of given bit
length; the measured $\approx 4\times10^{-5}$ bits suggests a strong bound is available.
\item \emph{The converse cost question.} Prove that any algorithm outputting the balance band of
$N = pq$ using only residue queries must make many queries; the seal shows one query class fails,
quantifying the failure is next.
\item \emph{Deep-position structure.} The family $\nu(M,k,t)$ agrees on an all-$C$ prefix; are
there witness families agreeing on an arbitrary prescribed prefix?
\item \emph{Interference proposals.} Claims of exact factorisation by ``interference of
Pythagorean triples'' in polynomial time are fenced by these results: such a method must smuggle
in the Fermat pair, hence be circular, or aggregate over a structure of size comparable to $N$.
Turning this into a formal impossibility statement for a precisely specified interference model is
an attractive target.
\item \emph{The quantum channel.} The research frontier moves away from the tree geometry to
quantum-channel questions and to the converse of the aggregation-cost barrier.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The address of an odd semiprime in the tree of primitive Pythagorean triples exists, is unique,
and is equivalent to the factorisation. It is nevertheless \emph{sealed}: its skeleton is exactly
the trace $N \bmod 3$ restated, and every other feature --- letter, depth, composition, at any
position, at any modulus, with any prefix given free --- is provably not a function of $N$'s
residues. The one direction in which the tree speaks is one in which we were already listening.

\end{document}

